EG001 missing-variable
──────────────────────

  Default severity: error

  A variable declared in a template or required by the schema is missing from an
  environment's real env file.

What it detects

  Each real env file is compared with the templates that apply to its
  environment: the app's default templates (`.env.example`, `.env.sample`, ...)
  plus the environment's own templates (`.env.production.example`).

  • A template variable is *required* if the template gives it a non-empty
    value, or if its line is not marked `# optional`. Only `KEY= # optional`
    declares an optional variable, and EG001 skips those.
  • A schema variable with `required: true` is required in the environments it
    lists (all if none), in app roots that reference it and in the root app.
    Template-based findings come first; a schema finding is not duplicated.
  • Local override files (`.env.local`, `.env.production.local`) are skipped,
    and so are environments without a real file: a missing `.env` is never
    reported.
  • With `--process-env`, the process environment is checked instead of the env
    files.

Why it matters

  Anything that reads the variable in that environment gets no value. That
  usually fails only at runtime, or silently falls back to a default.

Bad example

      # .env.example
      DATABASE_URL=
      LOG_LEVEL= # optional

      # .env.production
      LOG_LEVEL=info

      .env.production
        ✗ error    EG001  DATABASE_URL is declared in .env.example but missing from .env.production

Good example

      # .env.production
      DATABASE_URL=postgres://db.internal/app
      LOG_LEVEL=info

How to fix

  1. Add the variable to the env file named in the finding (`DATABASE_URL=`) and
     set its value.
  2. If the variable is not needed there, mark it optional in the template
     (`DATABASE_URL= # optional`), or narrow `environments:` of the schema
     variable.

How to suppress

  The finding lists the template declaration as a related location, so an inline
  directive on the line above the declaration in the template silences it for
  every env file:

      # .env.example
      # envguard-ignore EG001 -- only set in production
      SENTRY_DSN=

  A file-level `# envguard-ignore-file EG001` in the real env file (or in the
  template) silences the rule for that file. In `.envguard.yaml`:

      version: 1
      rules:
        EG001: warning   # or info, or off
      ignore:
        - rule: EG001
          path: ".env.development"
          variable: SENTRY_DSN
          reason: error reporting is disabled in development
